#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cabc1eeb-9608-5e71-8f52-686ab9f527d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328399} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd634da-f8ce-502c-8146-c98df7aedab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328400} {:answer/id #uuid "a91ce62d-50ff-5e6b-8bd4-db84f4c3ee69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328401} {:answer/id #uuid "cef42c49-bc5b-55eb-9d95-099a16363be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328402} {:answer/id #uuid "31bf39b8-f324-513e-86e0-acf8fd3305f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31bf39b8-f324-513e-86e0-acf8fd3305f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328403} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e291dc-eede-5cbe-8cb1-556d4ae6ff7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9337ccd-0531-5f2d-96df-8781c1aaac04" :question/math-academy-id "q-156690" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-x)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi }}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}]}
